1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
When does an index help? It depends on the query, the column, and the data.

2
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:10,200
Rows are stored in the order they arrived, not sorted by last name.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:14,266
With no index on lname, the only way is to check every row.

4
00:00:14,766 --> 00:00:23,866
Two rows match. Finding the first one did not end the scan: there could be more.

5
00:00:24,366 --> 00:00:26,366
The index keeps the last names SORTED, each with the location of its row.

6
00:00:26,866 --> 00:00:33,466
Creating it does not reorder the table. The table stays exactly as it was.

7
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:35,466
Sorted order puts both Barreda entries side by side: one small key range.

8
00:00:35,966 --> 00:00:39,266
SELECT * also needs id and fname, so each match is fetched from the table.

9
00:00:39,766 --> 00:00:41,266
Same two rows, different route.

10
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:47,966
On a table this tiny, PostgreSQL may still choose a Seq Scan. The win grows with the table.

11
00:00:48,466 --> 00:00:52,033
This query asks for every row. The index can't eliminate any of them.

12
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:58,733
Reading the table straight through is cheaper than walking the index and fetching every row.

13
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:03,800
A sorted list of LAST names can't find a FIRST name. Full scan again.

14
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:10,500
The name users_lname_idx is only a label. ON users (lname) decides which column is indexed.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,800
The table just adds the row at the end...

16
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:16,800
...but the index must put the new key in sorted order. One INSERT, two structures to update.

17
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:20,800
Deletes and changes to lname cost index work too. Keep an index only when the reads it speeds up are worth that.

18
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:28,400
PostgreSQL estimates the cost of each route and picks one. Any of these can be the right answer.

19
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:32,400
Compare the plan, rows, and buffers before and after, on the same query and data. Confirm the same results.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Remember: An index is a sorted copy of a column plus row locations. It helps when the WHERE clause uses that column and few rows match. No WHERE, or a different column: it can't skip anything. Every INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE also maintains the index. Don't guess: check the chosen plan with EXPLAIN.

